Q: CS50 Caesar : SEGMENTATION FAULT issue A: <p>In analyzing the code, the first task was to review the compiler output for warnings and there was a warning that pointed to the likely issue.</p>
<pre><code>||=== Build: Release in caesar_1 (compiler: GNU GCC Compiler) ===|
/home/craig/C_Programs/Console/caesar_1/main.c||In function ‘main’:|
/home/craig/C_Programs/Console/caesar_1/main.c|17|warning: ‘text’ is used uninitialized [-Wuninitialized]|
||=== Build finished: 0 error(s), 1 warning(s) (0 minute(s), 0 second(s)) ===|
</code></pre>
<p>Warnings like this usually point to undefined behavior, which can include segmentation fault.  With that some print statements were added around the statement in question.</p>
<pre><code>int main(int argc, string argv[])
{
    int i,n;
    string text;

    printf(&quot;Segment a\n&quot;);

    n = strlen(text);   /* Segmentation fault happens here  */

    printf(&quot;Segment b\n&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>With that, following is the terminal output performing a test.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/caesar_1/bin/Release$ ./caesar_1 13
Segment a
Segmentation fault (core dumped)
</code></pre>
<p>After moving the statement calculating the length of the input parameter to an appropriate location, further testing indicated that there were still other issues in the encryption/encoding process.  Following is a listing of the code with some comments pinpointing other issues.</p>
<pre><code>bool only_digits(string s);

int main(int argc, string argv[])
{
    int i,n;
    string text;
    string s = argv[1];

    int k = atoi(argv[1]);

    if (argc != 2 || !only_digits(s))
    {
        printf(&quot;Usage: ./caesar key\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }

    text = get_string(&quot;plaintext...: &quot;);

    n = strlen(text);                   /* Moved here - segmentation fault averted      */

    for(i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)
    {
        if(isupper(text[i]))            /* If lowercase, no encoding occurs             */
        {
            char r, z;
            r = text[i] + k;
            while (r &gt; 90)              /* If encoded character is &gt; 'Z', endless loop  */
            {
                z = r - 90;
                text[i] = 64 + z;
            }
        }

        if(!isalpha(text[i]))
        {
            text[i] = text[i] + 0;      /* Adding zero does no encoding                 */
        }
    }

    printf(&quot;Cipher text.: %s\n&quot;, text); /* Print out the resulting encoding             */

}

bool only_digits(string s)
{
    int i;
    int n = strlen(s);

    for (i=0; i&lt;n; i++)
    {
        if(!isdigit(s[i]))
        {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
</code></pre>
<p>As it currently stands, no encryption will ensue for lowercase characters, no encryption will occur will ensue with uppercase letters that have their value greater than the character 'Z', and if the adjusted value has exceeded the value of the letter 'Z', the program enters into an endless loop.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/caesar_1/bin/Release$ ./caesar_1 13
plaintext...: abcde
Cipher text.: abcde     &lt;--- No encoding occurs
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/caesar_1/bin/Release$ ./caesar_1 13
plaintext...: ABCDE
Cipher text.: ABCDE     &lt;--- No encoding occurs
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/caesar_1/bin/Release$ ./caesar_1 13
plaintext...: RSTUV     &lt;--- When a character value exceeds 'Z' the program goes into an endless loop
^C
</code></pre>
<p>With that, following is one version of refactoring to stay with the spirit of the project.</p>
<pre><code>int main(int argc, string argv[])
{
    int i,n;
    string text;
    string s = argv[1];

    int k = atoi(argv[1]);

    if (argc != 2 || !only_digits(s))
    {
        printf(&quot;Usage: ./caesar key\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }

    text = get_string(&quot;plaintext...: &quot;);

    n = strlen(text);                   /* Moved here - segmentation fault averted      */

    for(i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)
    {
        if (isupper(text[i]))
        {
            text[i] = ((text[i] - 'A' + k) % ALPHA ) + 'A';
        }
        else if (islower(text[i]))
        {
            text[i] = ((text[i] - 'a' + k) % ALPHA ) + 'a';
        }
    }

    printf(&quot;Cipher text.: %s\n&quot;, text); /* Print out the resulting encoding             */

}
</code></pre>
<p>Following some tests using this refactored code (FYI the value of &quot;13&quot; happens to work as a complementary encoding/decoding value).</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/caesar_1/bin/Release$ ./caesar_1 13
plaintext...: abcde
Cipher text.: nopqr
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/caesar_1/bin/Release$ ./caesar_1 13
plaintext...: nopqr
Cipher text.: abcde
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/caesar_1/bin/Release$ ./caesar_1 13
plaintext...: Hello
Cipher text.: Uryyb
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/caesar_1/bin/Release$ ./caesar_1 13
plaintext...: Uryyb
Cipher text.: Hello
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/caesar_1/bin/Release$ ./caesar_1 13
plaintext...: Hello &amp; Goodbye
Cipher text.: Uryyb &amp; Tbbqolr
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/caesar_1/bin/Release$ ./caesar_1 13
plaintext...: Uryyb &amp; Tbbqolr
Cipher text.: Hello &amp; Goodbye
</code></pre>
